# crt-bridge receiver @VERSION@

Pre-release: not yet tried on a PC outside the test bench.

A USB stick image that turns a PC into a crt-bridge receiver: it boots from the stick, runs from
memory, and shows on a CRT the picture an emulator sends over the local network. See
`README.md` for what the PC needs, and the `README.txt` at the root of the stick.

- By default only the built-in 15 kHz band is driven. The PC's BIOS shows its own screens at
  31 kHz before the receiver starts.
- Verified on one PC in BIOS mode, one AMD Radeon HD 7750 (`1002:683F`) and one JVC DT-V1710CG
  tube. UEFI boot is provided but not verified on hardware; Secure Boot must be disabled.
- 4 logical CPUs minimum.
- Writing the stick again erases `monitor.ini` and `settings.ini`: copy them first.

## Built from

| | |
|---|---|
| source commit | `@SOURCE_COMMIT@` |
| kernel | `@KERNEL@` |
| package snapshot | Arch Linux Archive `@SNAPSHOT@` |
| switchres | `@SWITCHRES@` |

Where each part comes from, and its license: `SOURCES.md`. Every package of the image:
`packages.txt`, attached to this release.

The protocol has no authentication and no encryption. Use the receiver on a local network only;
never expose its UDP ports to the internet.

